Black's king is seriously exposed around g8. The pressure comes from White's queen on c2 and the rook on d1. Enemy pieces are massing around it, the pawn cover is gone on the f-file, the shield pawn on the h-file has pushed too far forward, the g-file in front of the king is wide open and the back rank is airless. Meanwhile, Black's bishop on h5 is trapped — it has no safe square to run to. It is attacked by White's pawn on g4, and nothing defends it. A capture sequence here wins roughly a minor piece. On top of that, Black's knight on c6 hangs — nobody defends it. It is under attack from White's bishop on b5. Also, Black's pawn on b7 is overloaded — it is the only piece defending Black's knight on c6 and the rook on a8. White's knight on a5 is running out of squares — it has no safe retreat, and the trap is ready to be sprung. Meanwhile, Black's rook on a8 has been left without a defender — worth keeping an eye on.

White has the superior minor pieces. White owns the bishop pair. Black's f8-bishop is a problem piece, hemmed in by its own pawns on c5 and d6. White's g2-bishop is a problem piece, hemmed in by its own pawns on e4 and f3. The position is closed, with a locked center. Meanwhile, White has the healthier pawn structure. Black has a passed pawn on h4. White has a passed pawn on b5. Black's h5-pawn is isolated. White's a4-pawn is isolated. Black's pawns on f7 and f5 are doubled. White's pawns on c3 and c4 are doubled. Black's d6-pawn is backward. White's d3-pawn is backward. White has a queenside pawn majority. Black has a kingside pawn majority. At the same time, White is ahead on material. White is up the exchange. Beyond that, White controls the key lines. The d- and e-files are open. The g- and h-files are half-open for Black. The c-file is half-open for White. White has doubled heavy pieces on the d-file. White has a rook on the seventh rank. White owns the important squares. Black's position has holes on d5 and e5. White's position has a hole on e4. Black's knight is entrenched on e4. White's knight is entrenched on d5. Meanwhile, White enjoys a clear space advantage. At the same time, White is clearly ahead in development. Beyond that, White has the initiative and is dictating play.

A good plan for White: keep the position closed, where the knights outshine the bishops. Another idea for White: advance the queenside pawn majority. Another idea for White: blockade Black's passed pawn (key squares: h3). Another idea for White: double the heavy pieces on the open file. Another idea for White: use the extra space and sidestep piece trades. Another idea for White: open the position before the opponent finishes development.
